#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151956 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "2bcc7bb9-1b5e-5858-811d-1e5c14f0bc29" :question/math-academy-id "e-8288" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $6$?" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_n[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^n\\}\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}_6[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, \\: t^6\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_6[t])=6+1=7\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_n[t])=n+1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151957 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "e1ae146f-04ae-5662-9ea3-178a23b3c366" :question/math-academy-id "e-8289" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $9$?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{R}^8$\n2. $\\mathbb{R}_8[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8$\n3. $\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $3 \\times 3$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n1. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)=n$. Therefore, $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^8)=8\\neq 9.\\quad {\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n2. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{n}[t])=n+1$. Therefore, $\\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n3. Recall that $\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n}(\\mathbb{R}))=n^2$. Therefore, $\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{3}(\\mathbb{R}))=3^2=9.\\quad {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/id #uuid "6d2ba662-f58e-56ec-bf87-c1f26c12c1c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8290" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n1. $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$\n2. $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $50 \\times 100$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$\n3. $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomial functions in the variable $t$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $\\mathbb{R}^{100}$ and $\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n$\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^{100}) = 100$\nand\n$\\text{dim}(\\text{M}_{50\\times100}(\\mathbb{R})) = 50\\cdot 100 = 5\\,000$\nare both finite numbers.\n- $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n$\\big\\{1, \\: t, \\: t^2, \\: \\ldots, t^n \\big\\},\\quad n\\in \\mathbb N$.\nIndeed, we have that\n$\\sum\\limits_{i=0}^n a_i t^i = 0$\nfor all $t \\in \\mathbb{R}$ if and only if $a_i = 0$ for all $i=0,1,2,\\ldots, n$. Therefore, for any $n\\in \\mathbb N$, there exists a linearly independent subset of $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of size $n$, which imples that $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b61a755f-23e8-5039-b2f5-b5984fdb420c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Matrix Vector Space Given Its Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "61fa85c6-5b27-5f81-a7ba-363eb68e8d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8315" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, the vector space of all $5\\times 6$ matrices over $\\mathbb{R}$?" :question/worked-solution "The standard basis of $\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1$.\n\nFor example, the standard basis of $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$ contains\n\n$$\n3\\cdot 2= 6\n$$\n\nelements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHowever, we're asked to find the dimension of $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, not $\\text{M}_{3\\times 2} (\\mathbb{R})$.\n\nIf we were to repeat the same process above for the vector space $\\text{M}_{5\\times6}(\\mathbb{R})$, we would have $30$ matrices in our basis, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{5\\times 6}(\\mathbb{R})) = 5\\cdot 6 =30\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim}(\\text{M}_{n\\times m}(\\mathbb{R})) = n\\cdot m\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a8bf202-99c5-5bfd-8df3-5bd27cd65640" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimension of a Polynomial Vector Space Given Its Standard Monomial Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83ddb001-c66c-5cde-82ba-ae0b5587788e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149917} {:answer/id #uuid "a3391a57-058e-5e75-84eb-2a59d955d27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149918} {:answer/id #uuid "d0639148-b63d-5d85-b52b-ab06459c137b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149919} {:answer/id #uuid "11d9138e-a3ad-5b09-b5d6-41f0293a7839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149920} {:answer/id #uuid "41d24a0e-73ca-5d4d-ab73-1b5fcf42907c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d24a0e-73ca-5d4d-ab73-1b5fcf42907c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149921} :answer-field/id #uuid "79083a6d-fb71-5df1-b8a6-b6551be6ec1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149916}] :question/id #uuid "090e8cd2-3587-589a-a954-4b48d6d7a9fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-36471" :question/problem "What is the dimension of $R_{7}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $7$?"}]}
